            "The <strong>stages of change</strong> (transtheoretical model) say that people move through <strong>pre-contemplation</strong> (not thinking about it), <strong>contemplation</strong>, <strong>preparation</strong>, <strong>action</strong> and <strong>maintenance</strong>, with <strong>relapse</strong> as a normal part of the cycle, and that the right intervention depends on the stage: raise awareness with the pre-contemplator, explore ambivalence with the contemplator, agree one small concrete step with the person who is ready, support and solve problems in action, and plan for the lapse in maintenance. Telling a pre-contemplating smoker how to quit is wasted; asking what he likes and dislikes about smoking is not. <strong>Social cognitive theory</strong> adds modelling — people copy respected peers, which is why a mother-to-mother support group and a chief who is seen taking his hypertension tablets change more than a nurse's speech — and the central role of self-efficacy, built by practising the skill with success. <strong>Social norms</strong> and the <strong>theory of planned behaviour</strong> explain why the husband's, mother-in-law's and pastor's approval decides the woman's use of contraception. The <strong>socio-ecological model</strong> ties them together: behaviour is shaped at individual, interpersonal, community, organisational and policy levels, and effective programmes act on several at once.",
