            "In an outbreak the <strong>attack rate</strong> is the number who became ill divided by the number exposed, as a percentage, calculated food by food or well by well to find the source; the <strong>secondary attack rate</strong> is the proportion of household contacts of a case who become ill, a measure of how contagious the disease is. The <strong>case fatality rate</strong> is deaths among those who had the disease, as a percentage — how deadly it is once you have it (cholera under 1 per cent with good rehydration, above 5 per cent without; untreated rabies 100 per cent). The <strong>crude death rate</strong> is all deaths per 1,000 population per year; <strong>cause-specific</strong> and <strong>age-specific</strong> rates restrict the numerator or the denominator. The <strong>infant mortality rate</strong> is deaths under one year per 1,000 live births in a year; the <strong>neonatal mortality rate</strong> deaths in the first 28 days per 1,000 live births; the <strong>under-five mortality rate</strong> the probability of dying before the fifth birthday per 1,000 live births; the <strong>maternal mortality ratio</strong> maternal deaths per <strong>100,000 live births</strong> — the denominator that students most often get wrong. The <strong>crude birth rate</strong> is live births per 1,000 population; the <strong>total fertility rate</strong> the number of children a woman would have at current age-specific rates; the <strong>dependency ratio</strong> the number of people under 15 and over 64 per 100 of working age.",
