            "<strong>Screening</strong> is the presumptive identification of unrecognised disease in apparently well people by a quick test, so that those who screen positive can be investigated and treated earlier than they otherwise would be. It is secondary prevention and it is justified only when the <strong>Wilson and Jungner criteria</strong> are met: the condition is an important health problem; its natural history is understood and it has a recognisable early stage; there is a suitable, acceptable, safe and reasonably cheap test; there is an accepted, effective treatment for early disease and the facilities to give it; the programme is agreed on whom to treat; the cost is balanced against other needs; and the programme continues rather than being a one-off. Ghana screens blood pressure and blood sugar at outreach and wellness clinics, the cervix by visual inspection with acetic acid and HPV testing, newborns for sickle cell disease in several regions, pregnant women for HIV, syphilis, hepatitis B and anaemia, contacts of TB patients for TB, children with MUAC, and schoolchildren for vision, hearing and dental disease.",
            "A test is judged against the truth in a two-by-two table. <strong>Sensitivity</strong> is the proportion of people <em>with</em> the disease whom the test correctly identifies (true positives ÷ all with disease); a sensitive test rarely misses, so a negative result rules the disease out. <strong>Specificity</strong> is the proportion of people <em>without</em> the disease whom the test correctly clears (true negatives ÷ all without disease); a specific test rarely raises false alarms, so a positive result rules the disease in. The <strong>positive predictive value</strong> is the proportion of positive results that are truly diseased, and it falls as prevalence falls — the same HIV test that is almost always right in a high-prevalence clinic produces mostly false positives in a population where one in a thousand is infected, which is why every reactive screening test is confirmed by a second, different test before a result is given. The <strong>negative predictive value</strong> is the proportion of negative results that are truly disease-free. Screening tests are built to be sensitive, because a false negative sends a person home reassured with an untreated disease, and the false positives are caught by the confirmatory test.",
