            "An immediately notifiable disease is reported <strong>within 24 hours of suspicion</strong> by the fastest means — a phone call or message to the sub-district and the district surveillance officer — followed by the <strong>case-based investigation form</strong>, one per case, and during an outbreak a <strong>line list</strong>: one row per case with name, age, sex, address, date of onset, date seen, signs, specimen, laboratory result, outcome and vaccination status. Every facility sends a <strong>weekly summary</strong> of the weekly-reportable conditions, due early in the following week, and a <strong>monthly summary</strong> of the rest — tuberculosis, leprosy, Buruli ulcer, sexually transmitted infections, schistosomiasis, lymphatic filariasis, trachoma, hepatitis and the others — into <strong>DHIS2</strong>, Ghana's electronic district health information system, from which the district analyses trends and thresholds. <strong>Zero reporting is mandatory</strong>: if there were no cases of a notifiable disease this week, the return says zero. A blank return is not a report; it cannot be distinguished from a facility that forgot, and a district that cannot tell silence from safety has no surveillance. The district must also <strong>feed back</strong> — a monthly bulletin, a comment on the facility's data at the review meeting — because a nurse who never hears what happened to her report soon stops sending it.",
